    "title": "Why you might face IRS penalties if you mail your taxes on April 15",
    "text": "April 15, 2026, 11:34 a.m. ETThe deadline to file federal taxes is here, and missing it could mean penalties.Tax season, which began in January, is wrapping up. Filers who need more time can request a six-month extension which pushes the filing deadline until Oct. 15, 2026, but the request must be filed by April 15 to avoid penalties.Is it too late to send a return? Why you might face penalties if you mail your tax returns with the USPS.When is Tax Day 2026?Taxpayers have to submit their taxes to the IRS and pay any tax owned by April 15, 2026, according to the IRS.Taxpayers may also file for an extension, which allows an additional six months to submit a return, but any taxes owed are still due April 15. The federal tax extension deadline is Oct. 15, 2026.Can I mail my tax return on April 15? Why you many face penalties sending them via USPS on Tax DayFor the 2025 tax year (filed in 2026), the last day to mail your federal tax return is April 15.However, it must be postmarked by April 15. Dropping your return into a mailbox does not guarantee it will be postmarked that day, Herald-Tribune reports. Mail processing delays of a day or more could make your return late and subject to penalties.The IRS considers a tax return to be filed on time (even if it's delivered after the due date) if your envelope is properly addressed, has enough postage and is postmarked by the filing due date, according to the USPS. When you file late, you may get hit with a failure-to-file penalty. That penalty is 5% of the unpaid tax per month, capped at 25% of the amount due.To ensure it receives an April 15 postmark, you should go inside a post office and ask a clerk to hand-stamp the envelope, as automated mail processing may not postmark it until the following day. Taxpayers can stand in line at a local post office to get a hand‑stamped postmark, which USPS applies free of charge.What happens if I miss the April 15 tax filing deadline? IRS penalties explainedTaxpayers who miss the April 15 filing deadline should submit their tax return as soon as possible, the IRS notes. Filers may face penalties, which the IRS charges monthly until the full amount owed is paid.The standard penalty for filing late is 5% of any tax due for each month the return is late, up to 25% of the unpaid balance, according to USA TODAY. If you file a return but fail to pay any taxes you owe, or if you get an extension, you may face a smaller monthly penalty of 0.5% of any unpaid amount.There's no penalty if you were due a refund, but there may be a delay in getting your money, and you may lose eligibility for some tax credits. If you fail to file taxes repeatedly, you may be prosecuted for tax evasion, according to the Herald-Tribune.Already filed your taxes? How to check refund amount, statusTaxpayers who paid more than they owed throughout the year may receive a refund.Most refunds are issued within 21 days after an electronic return is accepted, the IRS says. The timing of your refund may change for amended returns, errors, or if you claim the Earned Income Tax Credit or Child Tax Credit.If you e-file your return, you can usually see your refund status within 24 hours using the \"Where's My Refund?\" tool.You can access your refund information for the current year and past two years. You'll need your Social Security or individual taxpayer ID number (ITIN), your filing status and the exact refund amount on your return.Refund status is shown in three stages: return received, refund approved and refund sent. It can take six weeks or more for returns sent by mail, and longer if your return needs corrections or extra review.When will Ohio tax refunds come?State refunds follow their own timelines, The Columbus Dispatch reports. In Ohio, you can check your refund status through the Department of Taxation’s online portal.",
